 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nHyperlink fragment and URL properties return incorrect values\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `fragment` property of hyperlinks is returning the address instead of the anchor, and the `url` property is constructing URLs with the fragment and address in the wrong order.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom docx import Document\n\n# Create a document with hyperlinks\ndoc = Document()\nparagraph = doc.add_paragraph()\n\n# Add hyperlink with anchor\nhyperlink = paragraph.add_hyperlink(\"https://example.com\", \"Click here\")\n# Manually set anchor for testing\nhyperlink._hyperlink.anchor = \"section1\"\n\nprint(f\"Fragment: '{hyperlink.fragment}'\")  # Should be 'section1' but returns address\nprint(f\"URL: '{hyperlink.url}'\")  # Should be 'https://example.com#section1' but returns 'section1#https://example.com'\n\n# Test with anchor-only hyperlink\ndoc2 = Document()\nparagraph2 = doc2.add_paragraph()\nhyperlink2 = paragraph2.add_hyperlink(\"#intro\", \"Go to intro\")\n\nprint(f\"Fragment: '{hyperlink2.fragment}'\")  # Should be 'intro' but returns empty\nprint(f\"URL: '{hyperlink2.url}'\")  # Should be '#intro' but returns 'intro'\n```\n\nThe fragment property should return the anchor value, not the address, and the URL should be formatted as `address#fragment` when both are present, not `fragment#address`.\n</issue>\n",
